#!/bin/sh
# guard CMD...: runs CMD with the test scripts' hang guard and exits with
# its status. The guard is not a wait for a result: it only stops a program
# that never ends. It bounds CPU time, not wall time, so a legitimate
# program slowed down by other checks running beside it is not killed; a
# Bend program that never ends spins and uses the CPU it is denied.
#
# 5 s mirrors Bend's own gate for its test programs
# (vendor/bend/gates/test.ts:136). The limit (RLIMIT_CPU) is inherited by
# CMD and counts the CPU of all its threads, also under Node and Bun.
# Observed 2026-10-16 on an Apple arm64 Mac (macOS, bash as sh): a native
# loop, Node and Bun spinning past the limit all die by SIGXCPU, exit 152
# (128 + 24). A program that ignores SIGXCPU is not stopped (macOS does not
# kill at the hard limit); none of the guarded programs does.
#
# Callers report exit 152 as "timeout", never as a mismatch.
ulimit -t 5 || exit 1
exec "$@"
